  "query_preview": "..\nChanges from last discussion - A Wind Adv has been hoisted for \nSW Pinal, central/western Pima Co from 2AM Sunday until 11PM \nSunday night. The other change were to increase coverage of \nshowers/storms this afternoon. Overall guidance, including \nCAMs, show an upward trend in convection. The main area would be\nfrom southern Pinal Co south to the border. Only expecting \nlight rain amounts, but brief heavy rain is possible under \ncores with wind gusts around 35-40 mph. \n\nAs of 200AM Rachel is now a Tropical Storm about 840mi/1350km \nSW of San Diego, CA moving NE 3 kts. Max sustained winds have \nbeen lowered to 50 mph, but may increase to around 60 mph as \nRachel could see some slight fluctuations in intensity as it \ninteracts with the upper level trof. Regardless of intensity \nchanges or slight track adjustments, our impacts remain the \nsame. For more information on Rachel or Hurricane Simon....see\nNHC.NOAA.GOV\n\nThere will be 3 general threats with the remnants of Rachel \ninteracting with an upper level trof. The first will be gusty \nSW/S winds from central Pima and southern Pinal west. Sustained \nwinds of 25-35 mph, with gusts 45-55 mph likely. These are not \nnecessarily accounting for any convection, which could pull \nstronger winds to the surface. Given this potential, the SPC\nhas placed locations from Tucson/Eloy west to Ajo in a Marginal\nRisk for severe weather. The main threat will be locally\ndamaging wind gusts to 60mph, with small hail, and heavy rain.\nThere is a non zero LOW tornado threat Saturday afternoon into \nthe evening. This would be a limited area of western Pima \nCounty, including Lukeville, Ajo, and Why. This does not \ninclude Tucson. Modest instability, plenty of shear, and \nincreased helicity in the 0-1/0-3km creates the non zero tornado\nthreat.\n\nLastly, there will be scattered to numerous showers and storms\nSaturday and to a lesser degree on Sunday. General rainfall\namounts across the CWA are 0.25-1.0\", with 1-2\" in the higher\nelevations. This storm total will occur over a long duration \ninto early next week. While widespread heavy rain is not \nexpected, there is certainly the possibility for localized \nhigher amounts. This is depicted in the CAMs, but confidence is \nlow on placement/timing. The most likely place for higher \nrainfall totals would be from Ajo to Tucson Sat aftn/eve. Lesser\namounts expected east of Tucson to the NM border. While there \nis no Flash Flood Watch, we will keep an eye out to see if there\nis a greater threat that develops over the next 24 hrs. \n\nFor Monday and Tuesday, the main factor for our weather will be the \ntrough axis to our west. There will still be another area of forcing \nand lift as a cold front moves through the region from late Monday \ninto Tuesday and that is likely to bring us more shower activity and \npotentially a few thunderstorms as well. Winds will remain gusty on \nMonday but not nearly as strong as Sunday. Drier air will then \nmove in Wednesday with any lingering precipitation chances \nWednesday to the east of Tucson. \n\nAbove normal temperatures through Friday will give way to near \nnormal temperatures on Saturday, then an extended period of below \nnormal temperatures will occur Sunday through the middle of next \nweek.",
